<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Windsor (1175)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1175">1175</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Windsor (1175) was agreed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland">High King of Ireland</a>, after the Anglo-Norman invasion. Ruaidrí accepted Henry as overlord and paid tribute, keeping Connacht and authority over Irish kings outside the Anglo-Norman lands of Leinster, Meath and the coastal towns. The settlement soon broke down as Anglo-Norman lords pushed further into Ireland.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was made during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland">Anglo-Norman invasion of Ireland</a>, at Windsor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a>, in the octaves of Michaelmas, around 6 October 1175. It was negotiated for Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair by Catholicus, Archbishop of Tuam, the abbot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonfert">Clonfert</a> and Master Laurence, chancellor of the king of Connacht. Among its witnesses were Laurence O'Toole, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, William, Earl of Essex, and the justiciar Richard de Luci.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Henry II granted Ruaidrí, as his liegeman, that he would be king under him and hold his land as fully as before Henry entered Ireland, so long as he served him faithfully and paid tribute. Ruaidrí was to keep the rest of Ireland under him and collect its tribute for the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>: one acceptable hide for every ten animals slaughtered. The other kings and peoples kept their lands while they stayed loyal, and any who rebelled Ruaidrí was to judge and remove, with the help of Henry's constable if needed.</p><p>Outside his authority stayed the lands Henry kept for himself and his barons: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, Meath, the whole of Leinster, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> with its territory as far as Dungarvan. Irish who had fled the barons' lands might return in peace, and Ruaidrí was to take hostages from those committed to him and give hostages himself. All Ireland was also bound by the religious provisions of the papal bull Laudabiliter and the Synod of Cashel of 1172.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The two kings understood the treaty differently. Henry saw Ruaidrí as his subordinate in the feudal order, paying him a yearly rent on behalf of all his sub-kings, while Ruaidrí saw himself as the restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland">High King of Ireland</a>, owing only a modest tribute; the Annals of Tigernach recorded that the kingship of all Ireland had been given to him. The treaty was soon broken as land-hungry Anglo-Norman lords expanded, and in 1177 Henry repudiated it and named his son John Lord of Ireland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Windsor (1175)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ruaidri Ua Conchobair, the last high king, accepted Henry II's overlordship in the <b>Treaty of Windsor</b> of 1175, after the Norman invasion and the creation of the Lordship of Ireland; he died in 1198.</blockquote><a href="/state/high-kingship-of-ireland">High Kingship of Ireland</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Ruaidrí became the last High King of Ireland and agreed the <b>Treaty of Windsor</b> with Henry II in 1175.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-connacht">Kingdom of Connacht</a></li></ul></section>
